# Native Busted unit-test migration

## Objective

Replace LuaUnit with native Busted specifications across the complete SoulSearch
unit-test suite without changing production behavior, losing coverage, or hiding
the four existing search-ranking failures.

The completed migration must:

- execute all 332 current tests through Busted;
- rename every discovered suite from `*_test.lua` to `*_spec.lua`;
- use native `describe()`, `it()`, and Luassert assertions without a LuaUnit
  compatibility layer;
- keep `Tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1` as the neutral public entrypoint;
- bootstrap exact repository-local test dependencies on Windows;
- preserve targeted execution, deterministic discovery, failure exit codes, and
  process-environment restoration; and
- leave no LuaUnit dependency, runner, suite structure, or current-state
  documentation behind.

## Architectural decisions

- Pin Busted to `2.3.0-1` in the ignored `.luarocks/` dependency tree.
- Pin `luasystem` to `0.3.0-2` and install it before Busted. Newer luasystem
  releases do not compile with the Windows toolchain currently used by this
  repository.
- Invoke the LuaRocks launcher explicitly with Lua, using the equivalent of
  `lua .luarocks/bin/busted`; do not rely on Windows executing the extensionless
  generated launcher.
- Add a repository-root `.busted` configuration for `*_spec.lua` discovery,
  deterministic file/test ordering, and stable plain-terminal output.
- Keep build/test tooling names, parameters, local variables, and custom
  environment variables framework- and project-neutral where reasonable.
  Framework names are allowed only where they identify the actual dependency,
  launcher, or configuration format.
- Replace `$LuaUnitArgs` with `$TestRunnerArgs`; forward remaining arguments to
  Busted unchanged so callers can use `--filter` and output options.
- Parameterize the test root, source root, and local dependency root with
  neutral names and defaults matching the current repository layout.
- Use only the standard `LUA_PATH` and `LUA_CPATH` process variables for module
  resolution. Preserve their previous values and restore them in `finally`.
- Rename `LUAUNIT_SMOKE_FORCE_FAILURE` to the neutral
  `UNIT_TEST_SMOKE_FORCE_FAILURE`. Do not replace `LUA_TEST_FILES` with another
  custom discovery variable; Busted owns final discovery.
- Perform the conversion through a temporary dual-run period. Residual
  `*_test.lua` suites run through LuaUnit and converted `*_spec.lua` files run
  through Busted until all suites have moved.
- Preserve each current test description, dynamic case, assertion intent, and
  fixture boundary. Busted suite prefixes may add context, but existing case
  descriptions must not be rewritten merely for style.
- Treat the current four `search` failures as baseline behavior, not as pending
  tests or migration work. Fixing their production behavior is a separate task.
- Preserve completed evidence in `docs/tooling-migration.todo`. Mark only its
  unfinished LuaUnit validation work as superseded by this plan.
- Add Doxygen-style comments to every new named helper, method, and class.

## Non-goals

- Do not change production Lua behavior to make converted tests pass.
- Do not diagnose or fix the four existing search-ranking defects here.
- Do not retain a LuaUnit-shaped adapter implemented on top of Busted.
- Do not mark known failures pending, skip them, filter them from the default
  run, or weaken their assertions.
- Do not add Busted, `.busted`, `.luarocks/`, test files, or test support files
  to the published mod payload.
- Do not rename the neutral `Run-UnitTests.ps1` entrypoint to a framework name.
- Do not require live DFHack validation for this test-only migration.

## Planning baseline

- Discovered LuaUnit suite files: 57.
- Runtime tests: 332.
- Current result: 328 passing and 4 failing.
- LuaUnit API calls to convert: 1,519 total, consisting of 1,518 assertions and
  the deliberate `fail()` smoke call.
- Current assertion inventory:
  - `assertIs`: 871;
  - `assertEvalToTrue`: 291;
  - `assertEvalToFalse`: 133;
  - `assertEquals`: 131;
  - `assertNil`: 83;
  - `assertAlmostEquals`: 4;
  - `assertStrContains`: 2;
  - `assertNotStrContains`: 2;
  - `assertNotNil`: 1; and
  - `fail`: 1.
- Existing failures in `Tests/search_test.lua`:
  - `ranking: earlier selected filter receives priority bonus`;
  - `ranking: full and partial matches remain in results`;
  - `ranking: mixed stat kinds evaluate together`; and
  - `ranking: raw score breaks an exact weighted-score tie`.

## Assertion conversion contract

- `luaunit.assertIs(expected, actual)` becomes
  `assert.are.equal(expected, actual)` to retain Lua `==`/identity semantics.
- `luaunit.assertEquals(expected, actual)` becomes
  `assert.are.same(expected, actual)` to retain recursive table comparison.
- `luaunit.assertEvalToTrue(value)` becomes `assert.is_truthy(value)`.
- `luaunit.assertEvalToFalse(value)` becomes `assert.is_falsy(value)`.
- `luaunit.assertNil(value)` becomes `assert.is_nil(value)`.
- `luaunit.assertNotNil(value)` becomes `assert.is_not_nil(value)`.
- `luaunit.assertAlmostEquals(expected, actual)` becomes an `assert.near`
  assertion with the current LuaUnit epsilon supplied explicitly; do not adopt
  a different Busted default tolerance accidentally.
- Replace substring assertions with explicit plain-string `string.find(..., 1,
  true)` assertions so Lua pattern characters cannot change their meaning.
- Replace the single deliberate `luaunit.fail()` smoke path with an explicit
  Busted assertion failure carrying the same diagnostic intent.
- Preserve any custom assertion message and verify that expected and actual
  values remain in the same semantic positions.

