## Phase 1 - Establish the repository-local Busted runner

☑ Add `.busted` with `Tests`-appropriate `*_spec.lua` discovery, deterministic
  sorted files/tests, and plain-terminal output.
☑ Extend `Tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1` with neutral test-root, source-root, and
  dependency-root parameters while retaining the current defaults.
☑ Rename `$LuaUnitArgs` to `$TestRunnerArgs` and preserve remaining-argument
  passthrough.
☑ Probe for exact `luasystem 0.3.0-2` and Busted `2.3.0-1` installations in the
  selected dependency root.
☑ Install `luasystem 0.3.0-2` first and then Busted `2.3.0-1` when either exact
  dependency is absent; fail clearly on an unsuccessful install.
☑ Construct dependency, test, and production entries for `LUA_PATH`.
☑ Construct the repository-local native-module entries for `LUA_CPATH` so
  luasystem can load its compiled module.
☑ Preserve and restore the caller's `LUA_PATH` and `LUA_CPATH` values in all
  success and failure paths.
☑ Invoke the extensionless Busted launcher through the selected Lua executable
  instead of relying on its file association.
☑ Keep failure handling host-safe: capture the child exit code, restore the
  environment, and then throw from PowerShell without terminating the caller's
  host.
☑ Add `Tests/busted_setup_spec.lua` with native Busted cases proving framework
  loading, repository/source resolution, discovery, and the neutral opt-in
  failure path.
☑ Verify a clean temporary dependency-root bootstrap, a warm repeat run, one
  `--filter` case, and the forced smoke failure.

Exit gate: the Busted smoke spec can bootstrap and run from any working
directory through `Tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1`, and failures propagate only after
the process environment is restored.

Phase 1 recorded evidence (2026-07-19):

- `.busted` selects `_spec%.lua$`, `plainTerminal`, sorted files, and sorted
  tests. With the 57 legacy `*_test.lua` suites still intentionally reserved
  for Phase 2, default discovery ran only `Tests/busted_setup_spec.lua` and
  reported `3 successes / 0 failures / 0 errors / 0 pending`.
- `Tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1` now exposes neutral `TestRoot`, `SourceRoot`, and
  `DependencyRoot` parameters plus `$TestRunnerArgs`. Positional binding is
  disabled so an unrecognized token such as `--filter=loads the framework`
  reaches Busted instead of being consumed as a path parameter.
- A one-pass clean bootstrap ran from the sibling `CommonSense` working
  directory against the absent temporary tree
  `SoulSearch-Busted-Phase1-LuaRoot-20260719`. The runner created the tree,
  installed `luasystem 0.3.0-2` first, installed Busted `2.3.0-1` second with
  its dependencies, created the extensionless launcher, and passed all three
  smoke cases.
- Exact post-bootstrap probes with LuaRocks confirmed both requested versions.
  The tree contained native `system/core.dll`, `term/core.dll`, and `lfs.dll`
  modules under `lib/lua/5.4`, proving the configured `LUA_CPATH` layout.
- This machine's global LuaRocks configuration incorrectly identifies
  `luarocks.exe` as its Lua interpreter. The runner now passes the selected
  Lua root and version explicitly to every LuaRocks probe/install. A clean run
  proved that this removes the erroneous `luarocks.exe -e` diagnostics.
- Correctly configured LuaRocks generates `bin/busted.bat` on Windows. To
  preserve the phase's host-independent launch contract, the runner copies the
  installed rock's extensionless command script to `bin/busted` when needed,
  then invokes that script explicitly through the selected `lua.exe`.
- A warm repeat against the clean tree emitted no install messages and again
  passed 3/3. The verified temporary tree was then removed.
- A default-root clean bootstrap installed the same exact pins into ignored
  `.luarocks/`; its warm repeat passed 3/3 without reinstalling anything.
- An arbitrary-working-directory run from `CommonSense` using default roots
  and `--filter=resolves the repository` passed exactly 1/1. A separate run
  with explicit absolute values for all three neutral root parameters and
  `--filter=discovered only` also passed exactly 1/1.
- With `UNIT_TEST_SMOKE_FORCE_FAILURE=1` and `--filter=propagates`, Busted
  reported the intentional one-test failure and returned 1. The PowerShell
  runner caught that code, restored its environment, and threw
  `Unit tests failed with exit code 1.` without terminating the caller host.
- Sentinel probes around both a targeted success and the forced failure
  reported `lua_path_restored=True` and `lua_cpath_restored=True`.
- PowerShell parsing, direct Lua parsing of `.busted` and the smoke spec, and
  `git diff --check` passed. `Tools/Build.ps1 -LiveReload:$false` passed with
  Lua 5.4.6 for 66 production and 65 test/support files. The package-tool
  contract suite returned 0; its malformed-Lua diagnostic remained an expected
  negative-path result.
- `Tests/run.lua`, `Tests/luaunit_setup_test.lua`, and every other LuaUnit suite
  remain unchanged for the Phase 2 dual-framework bridge.

## Phase 2 - Add temporary dual-framework validation

☐ Keep `Tests/run.lua` and LuaUnit installation available only for residual
  `*_test.lua` suites during the conversion.
☐ Make the temporary runner discover residual LuaUnit suites and Busted specs
  as disjoint sets by filename convention.
☐ Run both sets sequentially even when the first framework reports a test
  failure; capture each exit code and report the aggregate result afterward.
☐ Ensure the Busted setup spec replaces, rather than duplicates, the three
  cases in `luaunit_setup_test.lua`.
☐ Remove `luaunit_setup_test.lua` after the Busted smoke cases prove parity.
☐ Add temporary reporting that exposes residual suite count, converted suite
  count, total test count, and failures by name.
☐ Verify the aggregate run still contains exactly 57 suites and 332 tests after
  the first rename.
☐ Verify targeted Busted execution and direct residual LuaUnit diagnostics are
  both available during migration without becoming permanent public APIs.

Exit gate: one suite runs natively under Busted, all remaining suites run under
LuaUnit, and the aggregate result has no omissions, duplicates, or changed
failure identities.

